The only thing you also want to know is your radio receiver, and you look at the-- and you look at how much has it gone off, how much has the signal increased next to the sky. You have the same thing in the sky. Where you're looking, and you say, there-- there's the light-- dark spot, and there's the light spot.

Now, in the daytime, you can't see the stars, because there's all kind of light near it. But at night, that goes away. But if you're in a radio, it doesn't go away.

And that-- if it's a radio background or whatever it is, it doesn't go away that way. You'd have to make sure that it's not the antenna's pointing-- some part of the antenna's pointing the other way, something is dirty in your antenna, whatever the other reasons are for you getting this. But you-- you want to look for these small-- these small differences. And that's how we-- and that's how you do radio astronomy.

